<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Beas is a river of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, one of the five rivers that give the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> its name, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenab">Chenab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a>. It rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> and joins the Sutlej. Its waters sustained the intensive wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> farming on which the revenues of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> depended.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beas.jpg" alt="Beas" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Jupitus Smart · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beas_River_at_Kullu,_Himachal_Pradesh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
